y i got 11 players to play  test the game on  wednesday i got one more player to play  test um  based on some of the feedback we got and  oh by the way  everyone said that it was an interesting  experience and some of them even said it  was fun  a lot of them did say it was fun um  so that's good yeah so one of the first  things that popped up a lot was distance  detection or radar so you have no idea  how close you are to the wall you  experience that or you don't know how  close or far something might be we're  thinking of adding something like  perhaps you could have something like a  cane tapping and like you'll hear  different noises or oh there's a gap  here and we could take away barriers  around edges with the something like  this or something like rear assist  parking so as you like get close to wall  goes  yep  frame of reference oh yeah so the first  level went through a couple of  iterations in our most recent iteration  you have to find multiple targets before  you progress to the next level and this  is to give the player a chance to really  like  get more in the game understand this is  the target this is the sound the target  makes this is how i locate that sound  this is how i move forward and moving  backwards is also an option  the target after after the one that you  saw was directly behind  so the idea was trying to give players  that range of motion  and the noises was i listening to more  than one target at the time no no okay  target voices  that was just one target it was one  target yes the target would spawn after  the next one was destroyed that last one  was destroyed  yep  uh  yeah so front or behind versus left to  right  um  damage death and other punishments  so you know we talked about like players  spiraling into the void after they jump  uh what do you do when a player dies you  put them at the the beginning of the  level right  well  this is confusing because players don't  really know why they failed they know  where they are now yeah because now  you've just moved them without them like  moving themselves so they've completely  lost  where they are so we decided to take out  damage and dying and  all of that  we have a level with obstacles in it if  you hit the obstacle you say ouch you  get pushed back a little bit and this  doesn't disorient players if players are  worried about dying they're much more  afraid to like move and explore their  world as well so we took that out  level complexity  um as i mentioned during the play tests  uh players were saying that as as they  were going through they're getting  better at navigating they're feeling  more confident and they're getting more  used to the sounds they were hearing uh  so they're able to detect different  noises better and the patterns so we're  wondering how far we can go with this if  we have people that know how to play the  game how many levels of complexity can  we add can we have a staircase of  platforms can we make it so you need to  navigate almost kind of platforms almost  like a maze what can we do with this can  we have enemies how many enemies what  kind of things can be used uh so the  idea is really how complex can these end  up being made this was not something  that we could follow in the scope of  this class but that we would like be  very interested in pursuing and on  average it took players about 10 minutes  to get through all of the levels that we  had the three levels we only saw the  first one in this round but there's a  level with a platform and a long hallway  with swinging blades  yep  yes  and last level guidelines these are we  have three main level guidelines that we  want that we take as our contributions  and we want to give to you as main  takeaways from our project  the first one being to offer the player  one audio target at a time that can be  trying to navigate looking for a source  that can be trying to jump on the  platform that can be trying to find the  swinging blade  after you have gotten past that you can  present a new target as long as the old  one has been removed  second  always have a linear and flat path to  follow at least especially in the  beginning because you can't really  detect when something is above or below  you especially if you haven't heard the  sound before  so especially with these beginning  levels make sure things are just  in front of the player so this doesn't  mean that you can only move forward in a  level but it means you need to have a  linear progression path so you can like  you can go backwards you can move around  but if you've used an element that needs  to go away or else be specifically re  specifically brought back for reuse  yep  oh  and third give clear and immediate  feedback when you're looking at  something you're able to see  oh i missed the platformer oh i got hit  by something or oh i didn't make the  jump or oh i ran to a wall but if you  don't have that immediate feedback here  people are just like what happened i  it doesn't sound like anything happened  or oh things sound further away now they  don't have a good orientation point so  footsteps when you're actually walking  the nope when you can't jump the bump  against the wall when you hit it we're  very clear and good ways to give that  feedback  and okay well that's our presentation  it's our game going in blind yes  [Applause]  you
 

